    "text": "The circuit shown in the figure consists of two resistances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> &amp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> connected to two ideal voltmetres <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> &amp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>. Assume that a voltmeter reads <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∆</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><munderover><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></munderover><mover><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">E</mi><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">→</mo></mover><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mover><mi>l</mi><mo>→</mo></mover></math> between its terminals. A time-varying magnetic field&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>t</mi></math>&nbsp;(where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> is a positive constant of proper dimensions and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi></math> is time) exists in a circular region of radius a and it is directed into the plane of the figure. The reading of voltmeter&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/ci/image17-13c290f2-64d0-42fb-bb2d-4b720a15eee2.jpg\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"height: 139px; width: 250px;\" />",
